                                      ASK Redesign/Mk.II

Objective: Redesign the ASK Flowchart to properly accomodate for unidentified and uncompensated fall-through problems found
            in the first ASK Flowchart, such as sending and receiving ACKs, defining what Masters are and do

Why:  The first flowchart did what it was meant to do--get a general idea of how the process would look like. Since we
      ideally want this to look at "point-to-point" as possible, I figured the asking planetoid would hold most of the sending
      logic and thus would be a good point to start at for the flowcharts. The other flowcharts will revolve around the ASKs.

The original design: [Insert picture here]


Pros:  First design, gives us something to work with and plan from/to.

Flaws:  Glaring flaws.
        - What if request word is lost? (how to confirm?)
        - Master 2 is a local escape but doesn't let Main know it escaped
        - If we send ACK after payload, but ACK is lost, we exit before Main knows
            payload was received


The new design: [Insert picture here]


Pros:  - Accomodates for time slotting
          - Should disallow multiple requests from confusing Relays or Main
       - The first ASK flowchart assumed if not Master 1 and not suppressed, then we could
          Ask and then go ahead and send addresses with no affirmation. ASK Mk.II accomodates for the
          option of Main sending an ACK and only then we send address
      - Mk.II's request word waits for a compared request word to make sure the correct items were ordered
      - Master 2 sends an ACK to Main to communicate that it escaped the transaction
      - Experimentally, States/ACKs 0-5 are listed on the left side of the flowchart
          - In order to create a method of avoiding mismatched states, I proposed that instead of ACKs
              traditionally being a 1/0, we make it based on the state of the ACKs, effectively separating
              ACKs from the main data line, but linking Main and ASK's current states together
          - Using this idea, we might use a 4 bit number with a combination for any particular state and if
              and ACK was receieved (so the current ACK state that is misaligned with the other side), we
              might send back a special code 0xF, until the correct ACK is received, that we already moved
              forward in states

Flaws:  - The flowchart is much longer
            - While expected, this means a lot more chance for error, meaning ACKs might not be enough
        - Currently, the request word is ~20 bits long, which describes exactly what category and selection
            we want to be sent (ie. bits 19 downto 16 describe the metals where 0x0 is NONE, 0x1 is Steel,
            0x5 is Aluminum, and so on)
            - The length of this single word transmission can easily be lost or be corrupted in transit
            - Testing and other ideas to be explored

Important Ideas and Questions: 
        - ACK system redesign
            - If we want to use a separate line for ACKs, we can have much greater complexity
            - Con is having more footprint, but it separates confirmation from the data line
        - Investigating a request resize to make short or less potentially volatile
        - Time-Dependant Scheduling
            - Restricting planetoid request to a slotted time to avoid mixed signals
        - Relay design
            - Although the "relays" have been put off for now, it seems they'll actually carry
                a massive role in processing, obviously relaying, and sending correct signals
            - Potentially, we could set relay stations' clocks to be only triggered when data is sent
                to it
                - Doing this means we need more bits to describe which relay (say, 3 bits for 8 relays,
                    unlikely, but relays could connect to relays, etc), then the 4 bits for the planetoid
                    address, which increases the complexity of ground and space logics
            - To be considered, we could have OUTGOING specific stations and  INCOMING specific stations
                - This would clear comms for to/from signals, but requires twice as much in terms of
                    launching another rocket, as well as changing some signal assignment for bit addressing
                - More research needed

Conclusion:  The redesign flowchart is still a massive upgrade from the first. Although it is clear there's still many problems to be solved, it gave me a clearer
